1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:14,147
The case that nearly ended Albert Wesker was not
built on a crime. That is what made it

2
00:00:14,167 --> 00:00:19,929
dangerous, and that is why it took four years
for anybody to notice it existed.

3
00:00:20,329 --> 00:00:26,821
SSA Owen Carrick, forty-nine, seventeen years in the
Bureau, had never in his career filed

4
00:00:26,841 --> 00:00:33,476
a report that said the word *impossible*. What he had
instead was a private habit, developed

5
00:00:33,496 --> 00:00:37,889
over four years, of collecting things
that did not reconcile.

6
00:00:38,289 --> 00:00:43,746
A plant in Newark with fourteen years of
depreciation and no purchase order. A campus

7
00:00:43,766 --> 00:00:49,416
in Wilmington whose permits were signed by a planner
who could not remember the meetings

8
00:00:49,436 --> 00:00:54,893
but recognised her own handwriting. Four towers
that appeared on four skylines in one

9
00:00:54,913 --> 00:01:00,499
weekend in 2029, each with a complete photographic
history. A payroll containing eleven

10
00:01:00,519 --> 00:01:06,297
thousand people whose school records existed and whose
schools had no yearbooks with their

11
00:01:06,317 --> 00:01:07,200
faces in them.

12
00:01:07,599 --> 00:01:09,939
And one frame from a Swiss loading bay.

13
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:16,507
Individually, each of these was explicable, and
Carrick could explain them. He had written

14
00:01:16,527 --> 00:01:21,320
the explanations out, one by one, and every
explanation was plausible.

15
00:01:21,719 --> 00:01:27,416
Together, they were not. That was his entire case,
and there is no statute in the United

16
00:01:27,436 --> 00:01:29,170
States code that covers it.

17
00:01:29,569 --> 00:01:35,899
He took it to his ASAC in September of 2033, after
the Ravenna outbreak, when Umbrella's

18
00:01:35,919 --> 00:01:42,033
approval rating stood at eighty-eight per cent,
which — as his supervisor pointed out

19
00:01:42,053 --> 00:01:48,599
to him with some sympathy — was approximately
eighty-eight points higher than the Bureau's.

20
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:53,410
Owen. Walk me through what the predicate is.
Not the wall. The predicate.

21
00:01:53,810 --> 00:01:59,966
Records fraud. Systematic falsification of municipal
and federal records across at least

22
00:01:59,986 --> 00:02:01,300
nine jurisdictions.

23
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:02,810
By whom?

24
00:02:03,209 --> 00:02:04,340
That's the question.

25
00:02:04,739 --> 00:02:09,812
No, that's the *case*. You have no suspect, no
witness, no victim, no loss, no complainant.

26
00:02:09,832 --> 00:02:14,792
You have a company that saved four million lives in
March and a theory that the buildings

27
00:02:14,812 --> 00:02:18,709
are wrong. Do you know what happens if I
sign this? Not to you. To me.

28
00:02:19,110 --> 00:02:23,810
I know exactly what happens, which is why I've
spent four years not asking you.

29
00:02:24,210 --> 00:02:30,752
The narrator wishes to record that the ASAC was not a
coward and was not corrupt. He approved

30
00:02:30,772 --> 00:02:36,749
a limited preliminary inquiry, sixty days, no grand
jury, no subpoenas, and he did it

31
00:02:36,769 --> 00:02:42,747
because he had known Owen Carrick for fifteen years
and had never once seen him wrong

32
00:02:42,767 --> 00:02:44,300
about something small.

33
00:02:44,699 --> 00:02:50,649
Wesker knew within nineteen hours, and not
because anybody betrayed anything.

34
00:02:51,050 --> 00:02:57,242
He knew because in 2030 he had bought a
four-thousand-Point dossier on a CIA officer and

35
00:02:57,262 --> 00:03:03,737
discovered that the System would sell him a complete
account of any living person's actions,

36
00:03:03,757 --> 00:03:10,020
and because since 2031 he had maintained a standing
order — sixty-one dossiers, refreshed

37
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:15,879
weekly, on every human being who had ever formally
asked a question about Umbrella.

38
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:19,109
Carrick's was the only one he
read himself.

39
00:03:20,259 --> 00:03:29,188
Dossier. Carrick, Owen T. F B I Philadelphia,
Supervisory Special Agent. Preliminary inquiry

40
00:03:29,208 --> 00:03:37,455
authorised, sixty days. Forty-one exhibits, no
subpoenas, no disclosures to press. He

41
00:03:37,475 --> 00:03:44,457
has been told to stop three times and has
not. He has not told his wife.

42
00:03:45,048 --> 00:03:48,097
The passenger had been
listening. It spoke now.

43
00:03:48,617 --> 00:03:55,069
Have him removed. A man like this is removed with a
telephone call and a budget line;

44
00:03:55,089 --> 00:03:58,572
you own nine senators'
fundraising committees.

45
00:03:58,972 --> 00:04:04,191
Yes, and the day I do that he becomes a martyr with
forty-one exhibits and a lawyer, and

46
00:04:04,211 --> 00:04:09,251
every journalist who has been circling this company
since Basel finally has a *story*

47
00:04:09,271 --> 00:04:10,502
instead of a feeling.

48
00:04:10,901 --> 00:04:12,814
Then something quieter.

49
00:04:13,216 --> 00:04:14,954
(very cold) No.

50
00:04:15,629 --> 00:04:17,884
I have not suggested anything.

51
00:04:18,285 --> 00:04:22,575
You have suggested it for four years, in a dozen
different ways, about a dozen different

52
00:04:22,595 --> 00:04:27,082
people, and the answer has been the same every time and
will be the same when we are running

53
00:04:27,102 --> 00:04:31,246
four planets. Nobody dies for being clever near me.
Not because I have a conscience —

54
00:04:31,266 --> 00:04:35,410
you of all things know I do not — but because a
corpse is a *permanent* document, and

55
00:04:35,430 --> 00:04:39,035
I have spent seven years making sure this
company has never generated one.

56
00:04:39,435 --> 00:04:46,101
The narrator notes, for the record, that this rule
held for seventy-seven years, that

57
00:04:46,121 --> 00:04:53,181
it was broken once, in 2110, in another star system,
and that Wesker regarded the breaking

58
00:04:53,201 --> 00:04:57,665
of it as the worst professional error
of his second life.

59
00:04:58,185 --> 00:05:00,277
Find out where he drinks.

60
00:05:00,677 --> 00:05:02,677
Owen Carrick did not drink.

61
00:05:03,076 --> 00:05:05,407
So Wesker went to his house.

62
00:05:05,807 --> 00:05:11,891
No security. No car waiting. He parked a black
saloon two streets away and walked, in

63
00:05:11,911 --> 00:05:17,996
a long coat, on a Tuesday evening, and knocked on
the front door of a house in Media,

64
00:05:18,016 --> 00:05:20,797
Pennsylvania at twenty to ten at night.

65
00:05:21,197 --> 00:05:27,016
Owen Carrick opened it and found the most
photographed man on earth standing on his step.

66
00:05:27,922 --> 00:05:29,392
My wife's in the kitchen.

67
00:05:29,791 --> 00:05:34,491
I know. I would rather she didn't hear this, and I
would rather you didn't have to explain

68
00:05:34,511 --> 00:05:38,161
me. There is a diner on Baltimore Pike that
is open until twenty-nine.

69
00:05:38,562 --> 00:05:40,372
(flat) Am I under some kind of —

70
00:05:40,771 --> 00:05:45,913
Agent Carrick. I am here because you are the only
person in the government of this country

71
00:05:45,933 --> 00:05:50,502
who has asked the correct question, and I have
come to answer it. Get your coat.

72
00:05:50,901 --> 00:05:56,603
The narrator will now tell you about a conversation
that lasted four hours in a booth

73
00:05:56,623 --> 00:06:02,527
at the back of a diner, that was recorded by Carrick
on a phone in his shirt pocket, and

74
00:06:02,547 --> 00:06:06,431
that Albert Wesker knew was being
recorded, and permitted.

75
00:06:06,951 --> 00:06:07,628
Ask.

76
00:06:08,028 --> 00:06:12,218
The Newark plant. Route 72. January 2027.

77
00:06:12,617 --> 00:06:17,136
It did not exist on the eighth of January and it
existed on the ninth. Three hundred and

78
00:06:17,156 --> 00:06:21,571
eleven people went to work there on the Monday. All
of them were manufactured over the

79
00:06:21,591 --> 00:06:26,110
weekend with complete lives, families, and memories.
One of them, a supervisor named Ray

80
00:06:26,130 --> 00:06:30,597
Dorsey, can describe a holiday in Ocean City that
never happened, including the rain on

81
00:06:30,617 --> 00:06:31,268
the Thursday.

82
00:06:32,108 --> 00:06:33,148
You're admitting it.

83
00:06:33,548 --> 00:06:35,811
I am *stating* it. Next.

84
00:06:36,211 --> 00:06:37,181
The towers.

85
00:06:37,581 --> 00:06:42,772
Four, in a weekend, in January 2029. Nine million
people per city had their memories adjusted

86
00:06:42,792 --> 00:06:47,591
so that the buildings would have always been there.
That is thirty-six million people,

87
00:06:47,611 --> 00:06:52,354
and the only lasting effect is that they are all
very slightly less curious than they

88
00:06:52,374 --> 00:06:52,971
used to be.

89
00:06:53,370 --> 00:06:55,500
The building in Wilmington.

90
00:06:55,901 --> 00:07:01,014
Fourteenth of September 2026, five fifty-eight in
the morning. I opened my eyes in it

91
00:07:01,034 --> 00:07:06,148
two minutes later and I had been dead for seventeen
years in a place that is not this

92
00:07:06,168 --> 00:07:06,511
world.

93
00:07:06,911 --> 00:07:09,781
Carrick's coffee had gone cold
in front of him.

94
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:11,949
Why are you telling me this?

95
00:07:12,350 --> 00:07:16,760
Because you have already worked out ninety per cent
of it and you are the only person

96
00:07:16,780 --> 00:07:21,503
who has, and because I want you to hear the last ten
per cent from me rather than construct

97
00:07:21,523 --> 00:07:23,380
it yourself over another four years.

98
00:07:23,779 --> 00:07:29,668
And then Albert Wesker leaned back in a vinyl booth
in a diner in Pennsylvania and did

99
00:07:29,688 --> 00:07:33,859
the thing he had planned since the
twenty-first of September.

100
00:07:34,379 --> 00:07:39,201
Now. You have it. Every word of it is true, and you
have it on the telephone in your shirt

101
00:07:39,221 --> 00:07:41,730
pocket, and I am not going to
take it from you.

102
00:07:42,009 --> 00:07:44,417
Tell me what you are going to do with it.

103
00:07:45,367 --> 00:07:50,922
The narrator asks you to sit in that booth for a
moment, because this is the trap, and

104
00:07:50,942 --> 00:07:56,497
it is the finest one in this entire account, and it
is made entirely out of the truth.

105
00:07:56,896 --> 00:08:02,516
Carrick could arrest no one; nothing described was
a federal crime. He could take the

106
00:08:02,536 --> 00:08:08,221
recording to his ASAC, who would hear the most
powerful man on earth calmly describing

107
00:08:08,241 --> 00:08:13,993
memory alteration and interdimensional resurrection
to an agent who had been told three

108
00:08:14,013 --> 00:08:20,229
times to drop the case, and who would order a
psychiatric evaluation before the tape finished.

109
00:08:20,249 --> 00:08:25,935
He could go to a newspaper, which would require a
corroborating source, of which there

110
00:08:25,955 --> 00:08:31,641
were none, and which would end with him on a podcast
in eighteen months with a wall of

111
00:08:31,661 --> 00:08:33,167
photographs behind him.

112
00:08:33,567 --> 00:08:38,997
Or he could do the thing that the evidence actually
supported, which is what Owen Carrick

113
00:08:39,017 --> 00:08:42,917
had been circling for four years without
letting himself see it.

114
00:08:43,437 --> 00:08:45,267
(very soft) There's no crime.

115
00:08:45,667 --> 00:08:47,217
There is no crime.

116
00:08:47,616 --> 00:08:52,164
There are three hundred and eleven people in Newark
who are — what? What *are* they? Are

117
00:08:52,184 --> 00:08:52,786
they people?

118
00:08:53,187 --> 00:08:58,796
And Wesker paused, and the narrator notes that this
is one of perhaps four occasions on

119
00:08:58,816 --> 00:09:04,619
which he answered a question because he found it
interesting rather than because he needed

120
00:09:04,639 --> 00:09:05,267
something.

121
00:09:05,787 --> 00:09:11,264
Ray Dorsey loves his wife. He is kind to his night
shift. He worries about his cholesterol.

122
00:09:11,284 --> 00:09:16,460
Whatever else is true, that is also true, and it
will be true for another forty years,

123
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:21,777
and nothing I have found can distinguish him from a
man born in 1979 except that I know.

124
00:09:22,177 --> 00:09:23,567
That isn't an answer.

125
00:09:23,966 --> 00:09:28,897
No. It is the only honest one available, and I have
thought about it more than you have.

126
00:09:29,297 --> 00:09:34,716
The offer came at half past one in the morning and
it was not a bribe, which is why it

127
00:09:34,736 --> 00:09:35,158
worked.

128
00:09:35,678 --> 00:09:39,723
I am going to grow, Agent Carrick. I am not going to
stop and nothing on this planet is

129
00:09:39,743 --> 00:09:43,741
going to make me, and in nine years I will be larger
than the government you work for.

130
00:09:43,761 --> 00:09:47,946
You have three futures. In the first, you continue
this case, and it destroys you in about

131
00:09:47,966 --> 00:09:52,011
eighteen months without inconveniencing me at all.
In the second, you close it, and you

132
00:09:52,031 --> 00:09:56,028
spend the next twenty years in a field office
knowing what you know and doing nothing.

133
00:09:56,428 --> 00:09:57,658
(flat) And the third.

134
00:09:58,058 --> 00:10:01,168
The third is that you come and do
this job *inside*.

135
00:10:01,568 --> 00:10:04,139
He put a single sheet of paper
on the table.

136
00:10:04,658 --> 00:10:09,195
Director of Internal Security. You will report to me
and to no one else. Your remit is

137
00:10:09,215 --> 00:10:13,699
every person in this company, at every level,
without exception — including Mr. Hale,

138
00:10:13,719 --> 00:10:18,361
including Dr. Marsh, including Ms. Adeyemi, and
including me. You will have unrestricted

139
00:10:18,381 --> 00:10:23,288
access to every facility, including three that are not
on any plan. You may look at anything.

140
00:10:23,688 --> 00:10:25,848
And what do I do when I find something?

141
00:10:26,249 --> 00:10:31,163
You tell me, and I will either fix it or explain to
you why I will not, and you will decide

142
00:10:31,183 --> 00:10:35,880
what to do with that. I am not asking you to stop
being what you are. I am proposing to

143
00:10:35,900 --> 00:10:39,459
be the only employer on this planet who
has ever had a use for it.

144
00:10:39,859 --> 00:10:43,999
Owen Carrick looked at the sheet of
paper for a very long time.

145
00:10:44,519 --> 00:10:48,584
You've made it impossible to say no
without becoming useless.

146
00:10:48,984 --> 00:10:53,343
Yes. That is what I do. You will get used to
watching me do it to other people, and I

147
00:10:53,363 --> 00:10:57,929
would encourage you to keep a note of every occasion,
because that note is the only thing

148
00:10:57,949 --> 00:11:00,454
in this company that will ever be
worth anything.

149
00:11:00,854 --> 00:11:06,902
He resigned from the Bureau on the eleventh of
November 2033. His ASAC signed the form

150
00:11:06,922 --> 00:11:10,783
with visible relief and told him it
was the right call.

151
00:11:11,183 --> 00:11:17,095
He kept the recording. It is still, sixty years
later, in a safe in a house in Media,

152
00:11:17,115 --> 00:11:20,653
Pennsylvania, and nobody has ever
heard it but him.

153
00:11:21,803 --> 00:11:30,292
Adversary recruited. Owen T. Carrick. Human. Loyalty,
thirty-four per cent. Role: Director

154
00:11:30,312 --> 00:11:38,707
of Internal Security. He has not changed his mind
about you. He has changed his location.

155
00:11:38,727 --> 00:11:41,449
Five hundred thousand Points.

156
00:11:42,038 --> 00:11:48,605
On his first Friday, Carrick brought the case file
to Arklay in a cardboard box: four

157
00:11:48,625 --> 00:11:54,959
years, forty-one exhibits, eleven hundred pages,
every one of them written by him.

158
00:11:55,359 --> 00:12:01,785
He burned it in the library fireplace himself, page
by page, and it took two hours, and

159
00:12:01,805 --> 00:12:08,083
Wesker sat in a chair on the other side of the room
the entire time and did not speak

160
00:12:08,103 --> 00:12:08,453
once.

161
00:12:08,854 --> 00:12:12,644
When the last page went in, Carrick
stood looking at the fire.

162
00:12:13,163 --> 00:12:18,524
I want to say something and I want you to let me say
it once and never mention it again.

163
00:12:18,923 --> 00:12:19,726
Say it.

164
00:12:20,126 --> 00:12:26,332
Everything you've done up to tonight has been good.
The test. The licence. Ravenna. I've

165
00:12:26,352 --> 00:12:32,417
read all of it and I can't find the bodies, and I've
*looked*, I've looked harder than

166
00:12:32,437 --> 00:12:33,336
anyone alive.

167
00:12:33,616 --> 00:12:38,419
But I know what it costs to build something that
can't be stopped, and one day you're

168
00:12:38,439 --> 00:12:43,299
going to want something that isn't good, and on that
day there will be no one left who

169
00:12:43,319 --> 00:12:44,206
can tell you no.

170
00:12:44,927 --> 00:12:50,415
So I'm taking the job, and I'm going to be the one who
tells you no. And when I do, you're

171
00:12:50,435 --> 00:12:55,557
going to hate it, and I need you to remember that
this is the night you asked me to.

172
00:12:55,956 --> 00:13:01,027
Wesker rose, and crossed the room, and looked
into the fire for a moment.

173
00:13:01,547 --> 00:13:03,062
I will remember it.

174
00:13:03,462 --> 00:13:10,839
The narrator notes that he did remember it, on four
separate occasions, in 2038, 2041,

175
00:13:10,859 --> 00:13:18,752
2047 and 2061; that he overruled Carrick every time;
and that he never once had him removed,

176
00:13:18,772 --> 00:13:23,913
which is the single most human thing
Albert Wesker ever did.
